1. Hub
Definition

A Hub is a simple networking device that connects multiple computers.

It does not know where the data should go.

Whenever it receives data, it copies and sends it to every connected device.

Think of it as someone shouting a message in a classroom.

Diagram
          Hub
      /    |    \
    PC1   PC2   PC3
How Does It Work?

Suppose:

PC1 wants to send data to PC3.

Instead of sending only to PC3, the hub sends the data to:

PC2
PC3

Both receive it.

PC3 accepts it.

PC2 ignores it.

Real-Life Example

Imagine a teacher shouting:

"Rahul, come here."

Everyone hears it.

Only Rahul responds.

The hub works exactly like this.

Advantages
Cheap
Easy to install
Simple design
Disadvantages
Slow
Many collisions
Poor security
Broadcasts to every device
Not used in modern enterprise networks
Cybersecurity Perspective

Because hubs broadcast traffic to every connected device, someone connected to the hub can potentially capture traffic intended for others using tools like Wireshark.

2. Switch

A Switch is an intelligent version of a hub.

Instead of sending data to everyone, it sends data only to the intended destination.

Diagram
             Switch
         /     |      \
      PC1     PC2     PC3
How Does It Know Where to Send Data?

Every device has a MAC Address.

The switch builds a MAC Address Table.

Example:

MAC Address	Port
AA:11	Port 1
BB:22	Port 2
CC:33	Port 3

Suppose:

PC1 sends data to PC3.

The switch checks its MAC table.

It finds:

PC3 → Port 3

The switch sends the data only through Port 3.

PC2 never receives the frame.

Real-Life Example

Imagine a post office.

A letter addressed to "House No. 15" is delivered only to that house.

The post office doesn't deliver copies to every house on the street.

The switch behaves the same way.

Advantages
Fast
Intelligent
Fewer collisions
Better security
Efficient bandwidth usage
Disadvantages
More expensive than a hub
Requires configuration in larger environments
Cybersecurity Perspective

Switches support features like:

Port Security
VLANs
MAC filtering
Monitoring (SPAN/Mirror ports)

These are widely used in enterprise networks.

Hub vs Switch
Hub	Switch
Broadcasts to everyone	Sends only to the destination
Layer 1	Layer 2
Slower	Faster
Less secure	More secure
Doesn't learn addresses	Learns MAC addresses
3. Router

A Router connects different networks.

It uses IP Addresses to decide where packets should go.

Example

Your home network:

Laptop
Phone
TV
Printer
      |
    Router
      |
   Internet

The router connects your home LAN to the Internet.

How Does It Work?

Suppose:

Your laptop opens:

www.google.com

Steps:

Laptop creates the request.
Request goes to the router.
Router checks the destination IP address.
Router forwards the packet toward your ISP.
The ISP forwards it through the Internet until it reaches Google's servers.
Real-Life Example

Imagine a courier company.

A parcel needs to go from Delhi to Mumbai.

The courier checks the destination and chooses the appropriate route.

A router does the same thing for network traffic.

Advantages
Connects multiple networks
Connects to the Internet
Chooses efficient paths
Supports security features
Can perform NAT (Network Address Translation)
Cybersecurity Perspective

Routers often include:

Access Control Lists (ACLs)
NAT
VPN support
Basic firewall functions
Switch vs Router
Switch	Router
Connects devices in the same network	Connects different networks
Uses MAC Address	Uses IP Address
Layer 2	Layer 3
4. Modem
Definition

A Modem connects your home or office network to your Internet Service Provider (ISP).

The word Modem comes from:

MOdulator
DEModulator
Why Is It Needed?

Your ISP sends signals over media such as fiber, cable, or DSL. The modem converts these signals into a form your router or computer can use, and vice versa.

Home Network Example
Internet

↓

ISP

↓

Modem

↓

Router

↓

Laptop
Phone
TV

Many home devices combine the modem, router, switch, and Wi-Fi access point into one unit.

5. Access Point (AP)

An Access Point provides wireless connectivity to devices.

Example

Your office has:

100 employees
Wi-Fi throughout the building

Access Points allow laptops and phones to connect wirelessly to the wired network.

Diagram
          Access Point

      📱   💻   📲

           |

        Switch

           |

        Internet
Cybersecurity Perspective

Wi-Fi security commonly includes:

WPA2
WPA3
Strong passwords
Network segmentation for guest users
6. Bridge

A Bridge connects two LAN segments.

It filters traffic based on MAC addresses so unnecessary traffic doesn't cross between segments.

Example

Imagine one office floor is split into two sections.

Instead of one large network, a bridge connects the two smaller LAN segments and forwards only the necessary traffic.

7. Repeater

A Repeater regenerates weak network signals.

Example

If a network cable is too long, the signal becomes weaker.

A repeater strengthens (regenerates) the signal so it can travel farther.

Real-Life Analogy

Imagine shouting across a large field.

Someone standing in the middle repeats your message loudly so people farther away can hear it.

8. Gateway

A Gateway connects networks that use different protocols or architectures.

It can translate data so systems that "speak different languages" can communicate.

Example

An organization may need to connect a modern IP network to a legacy industrial system.

A gateway translates between the two.

Real-Life Analogy

Imagine two people:

One speaks English.
One speaks Japanese.

A translator allows them to communicate.

A gateway performs a similar role for networks.

9. Firewall

A Firewall protects a network by allowing or blocking traffic according to security rules.

Example

Suppose:

A hacker tries to connect to your server.

The firewall checks its rules.

If the connection isn't allowed:

Blocked ❌

If it matches an allowed rule:

Allowed ✅
Real-Life Analogy

Think of airport security.

Passengers are checked before entering secure areas.

The firewall checks network traffic before allowing it into your network.

Types
Packet Filtering Firewall
Stateful Firewall
Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW)
Web Application Firewall (WAF)

We'll study these in detail in a later chapter.

Data Journey Through a Home Network

Suppose you open:

www.google.com

The path looks like this:

Laptop
   │
   ▼
Wi-Fi Access Point
   │
   ▼
Switch (often built into the router)
   │
   ▼
Router
   │
   ▼
Modem
   │
   ▼
ISP
   │
   ▼
Internet
   │
   ▼
